{
  "profile": "issuer-key-derivation/1",
  "description": "The composite key an issuer signs its base_certificates with, derived from the same 32-byte master seed as its operation-key tree. The key depends on the seed alone: the issuer key id is a label stamped into the certificate's signer block and never enters the derivation. Two implementations that derive differently give one issuer two signing identities, and certificates from the same seed stop being recognizable as the same issuer's.",
  "rules": {
    "p256": "material = SHA3-256(master_seed || UTF-8(\"issuer-cert-key/p256\")). scalar = (int_be(material) mod (n - 1)) + 1, with n the P-256 group order, so the scalar is always in [1, n-1]; written as 32 big-endian bytes. Public key: SEC1 uncompressed (0x04 || X || Y, 65 bytes), standard base64 with padding.",
    "mldsa65": "xi = SHA3-256(master_seed || UTF-8(\"issuer-cert-key/mldsa\")). FIPS 204 ML-DSA-65 KeyGen_internal with that xi. Public key: standard base64 with padding.",
    "key_id": "The issuer key id is a label only. It is carried in signer.id and changes neither component: the same seed under any id yields the same two public keys.",
    "default_key_id": "When no id is configured: \"issuer:\" + holder_key_set_root[0:32], where holder_key_set_root is the holder-keyset root of the issuer's own operation-key tree (leaf i's ML-DSA-65 key is KeyGen_internal with xi = SHA3-256(master_seed || UTF-8(\"op-key/\" + decimal(i))), for i in [0, tree_size)). The root depends on tree_size, so the default id does too; the key does not.",
    "domain_separation": "The two labels differ from each other and from \"op-key/<i>\", so the signing key is independent of every operation key derived from the same seed.",
    "not_key_derivation_1": "This is not key-derivation/1. That family fixes the composite key materialized from a ceremony seed (HKDF-SHA3-256, mod n, the seed itself as the ML-DSA-65 xi). The construction here is the issuer's and folds its P-256 scalar differently; the two give different keys from the same seed by design."
  },
  "why_this_is_a_primitive": "A divergence here produces certificates that verify: each one carries its own public keys in the signer block. What breaks is that the same issuer, running two implementations from one seed, signs under two identities, so a verifier that recognizes issuers by their public keys sees two strangers. No digest comparison catches that.",
